@doc """
Standard `Eq` trait declaration.
`Eq` models equality for values that support exact comparison.
Both operands have the same static type. A type needs a standard, explicit,
or derived `Eq` implementation; display text does not establish equality.
The unqualified helper `eq(...)` comes from this auto-imported
trait surface rather than from a compiler-only builtin shim.
Bare infix `eq` and `neq` use comparison precedence and left associativity.
Their reserved names cannot be used for variables, parameters, or fields.

## REPL
xldr(1)> eq(1, 1)
True
"""
@autoimport
deftrait Eq {
  # Return `True` when both values are equal.
  def eq(self: Self, rhs: Self) -> Boolean

  # Return `True` when both values differ.
  def neq(self: Self, rhs: Self) -> Boolean {
    Boolean::not(Eq::eq(self, rhs))
  }
}
